---
import Landing from "../layouts/Landing.astro";
import Playground from "../components/Playground.astro";
import HeroShear from "../components/HeroShear.astro";

const demo = [
  "// The validator you already use.",
  "const Person = z.object({",
  "  name: z.string(),",
  "  age: z.int().nonnegative(),",
  '  sex: z.enum(["M", "F", "X"]),',
  "});",
  "",
  "// It is also the wire format.",
  "const bytes = encode(Person, person);",
  "// → 8 canonical bytes, validated before encoding",
  "",
  "const back = decode(Person, bytes);",
  '// → { name: string; age: number; sex: "M" | "F" | "X" }',
];

const payloads = [
  { label: "shorn", note: "", bytes: 8, self: true },
  { label: "Avro", note: "own schema", bytes: 8, self: false },
  { label: "SchemaPack", note: "own builder", bytes: 9, self: false },
  { label: "Protobuf", note: ".proto + codegen", bytes: 11, self: false },
  { label: "msgpackr plain", note: "field names", bytes: 25, self: false },
  { label: "JSON", note: "field names + text", bytes: 35, self: false },
];

---

<Landing
  title="shorn"
  description="One-line drop-in binary serialization for the Zod, Valibot, or ArkType schema you already use. No wire schema, schema file, codegen, or second source of truth."
  headline="Your validator is the wire format."
  size="md"
  cta="Read the docs"
  code={demo}
>
  Already have a Zod, Valibot, or ArkType schema? shorn is a one-line drop-in:
  install it and pass your schema directly to <code>encode</code> and
  <code>decode</code>. No wire schema, schema file, codegen, or migration.

  {/* The argument itself, not a table about it: the same record as text and as bytes,
      and the shearing of one into the other. */}
  <HeroShear slot="figure" />

  <section slot="after" class="plain drop-in" aria-labelledby="drop-in">
    <h2 id="drop-in">Install it. Keep your schema.</h2>
    <p>
      shorn derives the wire shape from your schema; Zod, Valibot, or ArkType still
      owns validation behavior, transformations, and errors.
    </p>
    <dl class="setup">
      <div class="self">
        <dt>shorn</dt>
        <dd>
          <strong>Use your existing validator directly.</strong> Call
          <code>encode(schema, value)</code> and <code>decode(schema, bytes)</code>.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Avro</dt>
        <dd>
          Define and maintain an Avro schema, inline or in its own file, then create an
          Avro codec.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>SchemaPack</dt>
        <dd>Repeat the shape with SchemaPack's builder before encoding.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Protobuf</dt>
        <dd>
          Create a <code>.proto</code> file, then run code generation or configure runtime
          reflection.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    {/* The size comparison belongs with the setup cost, not in the hero: it is the same
        list of formats, answering the second question you ask about each of them. */}
    <figure class="sizes">
      <table>
        <caption>That same Person record, encoded by each of them.</caption>
        <tbody>
          {
            payloads.map((row) => (
              <tr class:list={{ self: row.self }}>
                <th scope="row">
                  {row.label}{row.note && <small> ({row.note})</small>}
                </th>
                <td class="count">{row.bytes}</td>
                <td class="bar" aria-hidden="true">
                  <span>
                    {Array.from({ length: row.bytes }, () => (
                      <i />
                    ))}
                  </span>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <figcaption>
        shorn matches Avro's eight bytes without adding an Avro schema.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <Playground slot="after" />

  <section slot="after" class="plain" aria-labelledby="fit">
    <h2 id="fit">Built for shared TypeScript schemas</h2>
    <div class="fit">
      <p class="in">
        Reach for it in caches, RPC, worker messages, and compact application storage.
      </p>
      <p class="out">
        Reach for something else for cross-language readers, self-describing payloads,
        or automatic schema evolution. <code>fingerprinted()</code> stamps the wire shape
        into the bytes, so a payload an older shape wrote is rejected rather than decoded
        wrong.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section slot="after" class="plain" aria-labelledby="wire-safety">
    <h2 id="wire-safety">Safe to put on the wire</h2>
    <div class="proofs">
      <article>
        <h3>Canonical bytes</h3>
        <p>
          The same value always encodes to the same bytes, so a payload can be a cache
          key or a content hash.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>Defensive decoding</h3>
        <p>
          Decoding bytes you did not write is where binary formats get exploited. Bad
          lengths, invalid UTF-8, and trailing data are rejected, with bounded reads.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section slot="after" class="plain" aria-labelledby="size">
    <h2 id="size">Smallest or tied in every raw fixture</h2>
    <p>
      Small payloads normally cost CPU, because you buy them from a compressor. These
      come from the schema, in the same pass that encodes the value.
    </p>

    {/* "100-event payload" meant nothing without the shape behind it. This is the
        literal fixture from bench/fixtures.mjs, so keep the two in step. */}
    <pre class="fixture"><code>{`Person  { age, name, sex }
Event   { active, actor: Person, id,
          metrics: { cpu, memory }, tags[], timestamp }`}</code></pre>
    <p class="quiet fixture-note">
      Four fixtures: a Person, the same Person with non-ASCII text, one Event, and a
      batch of 100 Events in an array.
    </p>

    <dl class="setup">
      <div class="self">
        <dt>25 ms</dt>
        <dd>
          <strong>to encode 100,000 events</strong>, 4.2 MB. Gzipping that afterwards
          costs a further 79 ms, three times the encode itself.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>up to 6.2×</dt>
        <dd>faster than JSON to encode, and up to 14.5× faster to decode.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>up to 77%</dt>
        <dd>smaller than JSON before compression.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="quiet">
      <a
        href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "")}/performance/size/`}
        >Read the results, caveats, and reproduction steps</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</Landing>

<style>
  .plain {
    padding: clamp(48px, 8vh, 80px) 0 clamp(36px, 5vh, 56px);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--shorn-rule-soft);
  }

  .plain h2 {
    margin: 0 0 10px;
    font-size: 21px;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .plain h3 {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .plain p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .proofs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 240px), 1fr));
    gap: 28px;
    max-width: 1040px;
    margin-top: 22px;
  }

  .setup {
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    max-width: 900px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--shorn-rule-soft);
  }

  .setup > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(120px, 170px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 24px;
    padding: 14px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--shorn-rule-soft);
  }

  .setup dt,
  .setup dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .setup dt {
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .setup dd {
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .setup strong {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .setup .self dt {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .setup > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 3px;
    }
  }

  /* The boundary drawn as a boundary: one rule down the side, lit where shorn belongs
     and dim where it does not. The sentences name their own side, so no headings. */
  .fit {
    display: grid;
    gap: 18px;
    max-width: 68ch;
    margin-top: 20px;
  }

  .fit p {
    padding-left: 18px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--shorn-rule);
  }

  .fit .in {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
    border-left-color: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  .fit .out {
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .plain ul {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .plain li::marker {
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .plain .quiet {
    margin-top: 16px;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .plain code {
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    font-size: 0.9em;
  }

  .plain a {
    color: inherit;
  }

  /* The fixture shapes, so "100-event payload" means something. A plain block, not the
     hero's code treatment: it is a shape sketch, not runnable source. */
  .fixture {
    margin: 18px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    font-size: 13.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-2);
  }

  .fixture-note {
    margin-top: 10px;
  }


  /* ---------- the size table, now in the setup section ---------- */
  .sizes {
    margin: 30px 0 0;
    max-width: 60ch;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .sizes table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .sizes caption {
    margin-bottom: 14px;
    text-align: left;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  .sizes th,
  .sizes td {
    /* 4px each side against a 14px cell is a 22px row, the rhythm the old
       gap-based version had, minus the rows that silently grew to two lines. */
    padding: 4px 0;
    font-family: var(--mono, ui-monospace), monospace;
    font-weight: 400;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* Right-aligned, the way a horizontal bar chart always sets its category labels.
     Left-aligned, the column is as wide as "msgpackr plain (field names)" and every
     short row opens a void between its name and its count: "shorn" at one edge and
     "8" stranded at the other. Flush right, name and count sit together and the bars
     still share a left edge. */
  .sizes th {
    padding-right: 20px;
    text-align: right;
  }

  .sizes th small {
    font: inherit;
    color: inherit;
  }

  /* The count leads the bars rather than trailing them, so the bars share a left
     edge and stay directly comparable, and the numbers stay a clean column. */
  .sizes .count {
    padding-right: 16px;
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .sizes .bar span {
    display: flex;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .sizes .bar i {
    width: clamp(4px, 0.45vw, 6px);
    height: 14px;
    border-radius: 2px;
    background: var(--shorn-mark-dim);
  }

  .sizes tr.self th,
  .sizes tr.self .count {
    color: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  .sizes tr.self .bar i {
    background: var(--shorn-ink);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 18px;
    max-width: 62ch;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--shorn-ink-3);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .sizes th small {
      display: none;
    }

    .sizes .bar span {
      gap: 1px;
    }
  }
</style>
